; a metal mixed with one or more elements (metal or nonmetal) at atomic level.
; the mixture changes properties: harder, stronger, more corrosion-resistant,
; lower/higher melting point, different electrical properties.
; 
; two types of alloys:
; substitutional: solute atoms replace solvent atoms in the lattice.
; requires similar atomic size (within ~15%). e.g. Cu-Ni (cupronickel).
; interstitial:   small atoms fit in gaps between large atoms.
; carbon in iron → steel. nitrogen in iron → case hardening.
; 
; important alloys:
; steel:        Fe + C (0.02-2.1%). harder than iron, less brittle than cast iron.
; stainless:    Fe + Cr (>10.5%) + Ni. Cr2O3 passive layer prevents rusting.
; brass:        Cu + Zn (5-45%). golden colour, corrosion resistant, acoustic.
; bronze:       Cu + Sn (3-20%). harder than Cu alone. the Bronze Age material.
; duralumin:    Al + Cu + Mg + Mn. strong, lightweight. aircraft structures.
; solder:       Sn + Pb (old) or Sn + Ag + Cu (lead-free). low melting point.
; amalgam:      Hg + Ag/Sn. dental fillings. liquid at room temperature.

